.TH SCOPEBRIDGE-RUN 1 "September 2026" "scopebridge" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scopebridge-run \- run a Python script against a Rigol DS1000Z-E, or replay a session log
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scopebridge-run
.RB [ \-\-usb
.IR DEVICE " | " \fB\-\-lan\fR " " HOST " | " \fB\-\-sim\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.I script.py
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B scopebridge-run
.B \-\-host
.I HOST
.I script.py
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B scopebridge-run
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR HOST ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.B \-\-replay
.I LOG
.RB [ \-\-client
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-timing ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Runs a Python script that uses the
.B scopebridge
module
.RB ( "from scopebridge import Scope" ),
with the module importable and
.B Scope()
pointed at the server. With
.BR \-\-usb ,
.B \-\-lan
or
.B \-\-sim
it starts a
.BR scopebridge-server (1)
for the script and stops it afterwards, unless one is already listening on
the port.
.PP
.BI \-\-host " HOST"
uses the server on another computer (default:
.BR $SCOPEBRIDGE_HOST ,
else
.B host
in the [client] section of
.IR ~/.scopebridgerc ,
else 127.0.0.1); it cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-usb ,
.B \-\-lan
or
.BR \-\-sim ,
which start a server on this one.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-replay ,
it sends the requests of a command log
.RB ( "scopebridge-server \-\-log" )
again, in order, and reports each reply;
.B \-\-client
replays one client's requests only,
.B \-\-timing
keeps the original pauses.
.PP
To use the module without
.BR scopebridge-run ,
add
.I @PYDIR@
to
.BR PYTHONPATH .
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
.RS
from scopebridge import Scope

with Scope() as scope:
    scope.single()
    scope.wait_for_trigger()
    cap = scope.capture(1)
    cap.save_csv("capture.csv")
    print(scope.decode("uart", tx=1, baud=115200))
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR scopebridge-server (1),
.BR scopebridge-term (1)
